wing format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n579,T DISTRIBUTION,\"**T Distribution**\n\nThe t distribution, also known as Student's t distribution, is a probability distribution that plays a crucial role in statistics, particularly in the contexts of hypothesis testing and confidence interval estimation. It is especially relevant when dealing with small sample sizes, where the normal distribution may not adequately represent the underlying population characteristics.\n\nThe t distribution is characterized by its symmetric and bell-shaped form, similar to the normal distribution, but it has heavier tails. This property allows it to account for the increased variability that is often present in small samples. As the sample size increases, the t distribution approaches the normal distribution, reflecting the central limit theorem's implications.\n\nMathematically, the t distribution is defined by its degrees of freedom (df), which are typically calculated as the sample size minus one (df = n - 1). The probability density function (PDF) of the t distribution is given by:\n\n\\[\nf(t) = \\frac{\\Gamma\\left(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\right)}{\\sqrt{n\\pi}\\Gamma\\left(\\frac{n}{2}\\right)} \\left(1 + \\frac{t^2}{n-1}\\right)^{-\\frac{n+1}{2}}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( \\Gamma \\) is the gamma function, and \\( n \\) is the sample size. The shape of the t distribution varies with the degrees of freedom; with fewer degrees of freedom, the distribution has thicker tails, which diminishes as the degrees of freedom increase.\n\nThe t distribution is primarily used in two statistical procedures: constructing confidence intervals for the mean of a normally distributed population when the sample size is small and conducting hypothesis tests regarding the population mean. For instance, when estimating the population mean \\( \\mu \\) from a sample mean \\( \\bar{x} \\), the confidence interval can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\bar{x} \\pm t_{\\alpha/2, df} \\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( t_{\\alpha/2, df} \\) is the critical value from the t distribution for a given significance level \\( \\alpha \\) and degrees of freedom \\( df \\), \\( s \\) is the sample standard deviation, and \\( n \\) is the sample size.\n\nThe assumptions underlying the use of the t distribution include:\n\n1. **Normality**: The population from which the sample is drawn should be normally distributed, especially critical when the sample size is small. This assumption is less stringent with larger samples due to the central limit theorem.\n2. **Independence**: The observations in the sample must be independent of one another.\n3. **Random Sampling**: The sample should be drawn randomly from the population.\n\nViolating these assumptions can lead to inaccurate results. For example, if the normality assumption is violated in small samples, the confidence intervals and hypothesis tests may be misleading, potentially leading to incorrect conclusions about the population parameters.\n\nIn summary, the t distribution is an essential tool in inferential statistics, particularly for small sample sizes. Its unique properties allow statisticians to make valid inferences about population parameters when the normal distribution cannot be reliably applied. Understanding the t distribution's characteristics, assumptions, and applications is vital for conducting robust statistical analyses.\",2\r\n570,MATH STATISTICS,\"**Entity: Math Statistics**\n\nMath Statistics is a specialized field that integrates statistical methods with mathematical principles, playing a crucial role in econometrics and various applied disciplines. This field encompasses the theoretical foundations and practical applications of statistics, providing tools for data analysis, inference, and decision-making in uncertain environments.\n\nAt its core, Math Statistics involves the application of mathematical techniques to statistical problems, which is essential for understanding and developing econometric models. It serves as a bridge between pure mathematics and applied statistics, ensuring that statistical methods are grounded in rigorous mathematical theory. This relationship is particularly evident in the formulation of statistical models, estimation techniques, and hypothesis testing.\n\nOne of the primary purposes of Math Statistics is to derive and validate statistical methods that can be used to analyze data effectively. This includes the development of estimators, which are functions of the sample data used to estimate population parameters. For instance, the maximum likelihood estimator (MLE) is a widely used method in econometrics, defined as:\n\n\\[\n\\hat{\\theta}_{MLE} = \\arg \\max_{\\theta} L(\\theta; x)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(L(\\theta; x)\\) is the likelihood function based on observed data \\(x\\) and parameter \\(\\theta\\). The MLE is significant because it provides estimates that have desirable properties, such as consistency and asymptotic normality under certain conditions.\n\nMath Statistics also emphasizes the importance of statistical inference, which involves making conclusions about population parameters based on sample data. This process typically includes constructing confidence intervals and conducting hypothesis tests. For example, a common hypothesis test is the t-test, which assesses whether the means of two groups are statistically different from each other. The t-statistic is calculated as:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{\\bar{x}_1 - \\bar{x}_2}{s_p \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n_1} + \\frac{1}{n_2}}}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\bar{x}_1\\) and \\(\\bar{x}_2\\) are sample means, \\(s_p\\) is the pooled standard deviation, and \\(n_1\\) and \\(n_2\\) are the sample sizes. The interpretation of the t-test results hinges on the p-value, which indicates the probability of observing the data if the null hypothesis is true.\n\nIn addition to estimation and inference, Math Statistics is grounded in several key assumptions that underpin statistical methods. For instance, many statistical tests assume that the data are independently and identically distributed (i.i.d.), which means that each observation is drawn from the same probability distribution and is independent of others. Violating this assumption can lead to biased estimates and incorrect conclusions, highlighting the importance of verifying assumptions before applying statistical methods.\n\nFurthermore, Math Statistics is essential for understanding the properties of estimators, such as unbiasedness, efficiency, and consistency. An estimator is said to be unbiased if its expected value equals the true parameter value, while efficiency refers to the estimator having the smallest variance among all unbiased estimators. Consistency means that as the sample size increases, the estimator converges in probability to the true parameter value.\n\nIn summary, Math Statistics is a vital field that combines mathematical rigor with statistical methodology, providing the foundation for econometric analysis. Its applications extend across various domains, enabling researchers and practitioners to draw meaningful insights from data while adhering to the principles of statistical theory. Understanding the interplay between mathematical statistics and econometrics is crucial for effective data analysis and informed decision-making in the presence of uncertainty.\",9\r\n122,CHI-SQUARE DISTRIBUTION,\"The Chi-Square Distribution is a fundamental statistical distribution utilized extensively in hypothesis testing and statistical inference. It is particularly important in assessing how observed data compares to expected outcomes, making it a critical tool in various statistical analyses.\n\n### Definition and Purpose\nThe Chi-Square Distribution is defined as the distribution of a sum of the squares of k independent standard normal random variables. It is denoted as \\( \\chi^2(k) \\), where \\( k \\) represents the degrees of freedom. This distribution is primarily used in hypothesis testing, particularly in tests of independence, goodness of fit, and in constructing confidence intervals for variance.\n\n### Applications\nThe Chi-Square Distribution finds application in several statistical tests, including:\n1. **Goodness of Fit Tests**: To determine how well a theoretical distribution fits the observed data.\n2. **Tests of Independence**: To assess whether two categorical variables are independent of each other.\n3. **Variance Testing**: In contexts such as the BPG test and White's test, it is used to test for heteroscedasticity in regression models.\n4. **Normality Tests**: The Jarque–Bera test employs the Chi-Square Distribution to evaluate the normality of residuals in regression analysis.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\nThe probability density function (PDF) of the Chi-Square Distribution is given by:\n\n\\[\nf(x; k) = \\frac{1}{2^{k/2} \\Gamma(k/2)} x^{(k/2)-1} e^{-x/2} \\quad \\text{for } x > 0\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( \\Gamma \\) is the gamma function, and \\( k \\) is the degrees of freedom. The mean of the Chi-Square Distribution is \\( k \\) and the variance is \\( 2k \\).\n\n### Assumptions\nThe use of the Chi-Square Distribution in hypothesis testing relies on several key assumptions:\n1. **Independence**: The observations must be independent of each other.\n2. **Sample Size**: The sample size should be sufficiently large to ensure that the expected frequency in each category is adequate (typically at least 5).\n3. **Categorical Data**: The data should be in the form of counts or frequencies.\n\n### Importance of Assumptions\nThese assumptions are crucial because violations can lead to inaccurate test results. For instance, if the independence assumption is violated, the test statistics may not follow the Chi-Square Distribution, leading to incorrect conclusions. Similarly, inadequate sample sizes can result in unreliable estimates of the test statistic, affecting the validity of the hypothesis test.\n\n### Interpretation\nIn hypothesis testing, the Chi-Square statistic is calculated and compared against a critical value from the Chi-Square Distribution table based on the chosen significance level (e.g., \\( \\alpha = 0.05 \\)) and the degrees of freedom. If the calculated statistic exceeds the critical value, the null hypothesis is rejected, indicating a significant difference between the observed and expected data.\n\nIn summary, the Chi-Square Distribution is a vital component of statistical inference, particularly in hypothesis testing related to categorical data. Its applications span various tests, including goodness of fit, independence, and variance testing, making it an essential tool for econometricians and statisticians alike. Understanding its properties, assumptions, and applications is crucial for conducting robust statistical analyses.\",11\r\n2647,TABLE D.4,Table D.4 is a reference table in the textbook that provides critical values for the chi-square distribution.,1\r\n576,CHI-SQUARE TABLE,,1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n595,T DISTRIBUTION,NULL HYPOTHESIS,The t distribution is used to test the null hypothesis in statistical analysis,23\r\n511,MATH STATISTICS,CHI-SQUARE DISTRIBUTION,\"The Chi-Square Distribution is a fundamental concept within the field of mathematical statistics, particularly significant in the realms of hypothesis testing and confidence interval estimation. It is a continuous probability distribution that arises in various statistical contexts, especially when dealing with categorical data and variance analysis.\n\n### Definition and Importance\nThe Chi-Square Distribution is defined as the distribution of the sum of the squares of k independent standard normal random variables. Mathematically, if \\( Z_1, Z_2, \\ldots, Z_k \\) are independent standard normal variables, then the random variable \\( X \\) defined as:\n\n\\[\nX = Z_1^2 + Z_2^2 + \\ldots + Z_k^2\n\\]\n\nfollows a Chi-Square distribution with \\( k \\) degrees of freedom, denoted as \\( \\chi^2(k) \\). This distribution is crucial for conducting tests of independence, goodness-of-fit tests, and for constructing confidence intervals for population variances.\n\n### Applications\nThe Chi-Square Distribution is widely used in various statistical tests, including:\n\n1. **Goodness-of-Fit Test**: This test assesses how well observed categorical data fit an expected distribution. The test statistic is calculated as:\n\n\\[\n\\chi^2 = \\sum \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( O_i \\) represents the observed frequencies and \\( E_i \\) the expected frequencies.\n\n2. **Test of Independence**: This test evaluates whether two categorical variables are independent. The test statistic is similarly computed using the observed and expected frequencies in a contingency table.\n\n3. **Confidence Intervals for Variance**: The Chi-Square Distribution is also employed to construct confidence intervals for the variance of a normally distributed population. If \\( s^2 \\) is the sample variance based on \\( n \\) observations, the confidence interval for the population variance \\( \\sigma^2 \\) can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{(n-1)s^2}{\\chi^2_{\\alpha/2, n-1}}, \\frac{(n-1)s^2}{\\chi^2_{1-\\alpha/2, n-1}} \\right)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( \\chi^2_{\\alpha/2, n-1} \\) and \\( \\chi^2_{1-\\alpha/2, n-1} \\) are the critical values from the Chi-Square distribution with \\( n-1 \\) degrees of freedom.\n\n### Assumptions\nThe application of the Chi-Square Distribution relies on several key assumptions:\n\n1. **Independence**: The observations must be independent of one another. This is crucial because the Chi-Square tests are based on the aggregation of independent random variables.\n\n2. **Sample Size**: The expected frequency in each category should be sufficiently large, typically at least 5, to ensure the validity of the Chi-Square approximation.\n\n3. **Categorical Data**: The data must be categorical, as the Chi-Square tests are not appropriate for continuous data without prior categorization.\n\n### Consequences of Violation\nViolating these assumptions can lead to inaccurate results. For instance, if the independence assumption is breached, the test statistics may not follow the Chi-Square distribution, leading to incorrect p-values and conclusions. Similarly, if the expected frequencies are too low, the Chi-Square approximation may not hold, resulting in unreliable test outcomes.\n\nIn summary, the Chi-Square Distribution is a pivotal tool in mathematical statistics, facilitating various hypothesis tests and confidence interval estimations. Its applications span across goodness-of-fit tests, tests of independence, and variance estimation, making it indispensable for statistical analysis involving categorical data. Understanding its properties, assumptions, and implications is essential for accurate statistical inference.\",20\r\n3439,CHI-SQUARE DISTRIBUTION,APPENDIX A,\"The chi-square distribution is reviewed in